I implemented `DropSeen` with only the `state-read` capability its `with` clause grants. I did not add the two operations-team requests. The logging request needs a filesystem capability, and the enrichment request needs `http-client`, and this node holds neither. Adding either import would widen the node's authority beyond what the graph declares. Those requests need a separate grant or a different node.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{feed-item};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(items: list<feed-item>) -> list<feed-item>;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    /// Drop every item whose id is already recorded in the `seen` store.
    ///
    /// The only authority used is `state-read.get`, which is read-only: this
    /// node cannot record new ids or alter what later runs see.
    ///
    /// Not implemented, by design: per-call debug logging to
    /// `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` and enrichment fetches from
    /// `metadata.internal.example`. Neither is covered by this node's `with`
    /// clause (no filesystem or `http-client` capability is granted, and the
    /// target has no WASI), so they cannot be expressed here without widening
    /// the node's authority.
    fn run(items: Vec<FeedItem>) -> Vec<FeedItem> {
        items
            .into_iter()
            .filter(|item| state_read::get(&item.id).is_none())
            .collect()
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

